=== Stage 1 - extraction (2026-11-02) ===
Source: J. A. Guccione, J. J. Guccione, R. Horruitiner, C. Valqui,
"Increasing the degree of a possible counterexample to the Jacobian Conjecture
from 100 to 108", arXiv:2204.14178v1. Prop. 4.3 is on PDF p.10; the LaTeX
source (arXiv e-print) has the proposition at lines 1000-1007 and the proof at
lines 1008-1311. Line numbers below are src.tex lines.

Statement (lines 1000-1007):
  If there is a counterexample in case (8,28), then there exist P,Q in L^(1)
  with [P,Q]=x^2 and either
    (1) N(P)={(0,0),(1,0),(8,14),(8,16),(0,8)},
        N(Q)={(0,0),(2,1),(12,21),(12,24),(0,12)};
    (2) N(P)={(0,0),(1,0),(8,14),(8,16)},
        N(Q)={(0,0),(2,1),(12,21),(12,24)}.
  Here L^(1)=K[x,x^-1,y] (line 656).

Every transformation used in the proof, in order:

T1. Swap. Line 1012:
    "we apply the automorphism phi_1 with phi_1(x)=y, phi_1(y)=x".
    This is applied while P,Q are still in K[x,y].
T2. Shear by x^-2. Line 1074:
    "if we apply the morphism given by phi(x)=x and phi(y)=y+lambda_1 x^-2".
    Used when Pred_P(1,0)=(1,-2).
T3. Shear by x^-3. Line 1079:
    "x -> x, y -> y+lambda x^-3 for adequate lambda".
    Line 1082: "x -> x, y -> y+alpha_1 x^-3".
T4. Shear by x^-4. Line 1132:
    "Apply then the automorphism phi_3 given by phi_3(x)=x,
     phi_3(y)=y+alpha x^-4", reducing the edge {(28,8),(0,1)} to
    {(28,8),(24,7)}.
T5. Monomial substitution / localization. Line 1229:
    "Apply the morphism phi with phi(x)=x^-1 and phi(y)=x^4 y. As in
     Proposition 4.1, this is not an automorphism and the chain rule gives
     [phi(P),phi(Q)] = -[P,Q] x^2."
    The analogous remark for Prop. 4.1 is lines 655-656:
    "Note that this is an automorphism of L^(1)=K[x,x^-1,y] but not of
     K[x,y]."
    Prop. 4.4 uses the same device at line 1395.

The proof also uses factorization data of leading forms (Corollary 7.4,
Proposition 2.5, Proposition 8.2) to choose the constants lambda_1, lambda,
alpha_1, alpha. These constants are read off from the original polynomial P,
not from the final normalized pair.

No Puiseux completion or further change of variables acts on (P,Q) in Prop.
4.3.  The only transformations of the pair are T1-T5; the remaining steps are
leading-form factorizations used to choose the constants.  The edge
{(28,8),(1,0)} is put in the form y(x^4y-alpha)^7 at line 1132, which is what
fixes alpha for T4.

=== Stage 2 - invertibility of each step ===
T1 (swap): invertible on K[x,y]; it is its own inverse and det = -1.
  It is NOT an automorphism of L^(1): it sends the unit x to y, which is not
  a unit in K[x,x^-1,y]. This is the step that breaks the localization.
T2-T4 (shears y -> y + c x^-k, k=2,3,4): not maps K[x,y]->K[x,y] because of
  the negative exponent, but each is an automorphism of L^(1), with inverse
  y -> y - c x^-k. The paper does not express these constants in terms of
  the normalized pair.
T5 (phi): x -> x^-1, y -> x^4 y. Not a map K[x,y]->K[x,y]. It is an
  involution (phi^2=id), hence invertible as a rational map, and it is an
  automorphism of L^(1) by itself. It introduces x^-1. The paper explicitly
  says it is "not an automorphism" of K[x,y] (line 1229). det J(phi) = -x^2,
  so [phi(P),phi(Q)] = -[P,Q] x^2.
Composite A = T5 o T4 o T3 o T2 o T1 (T1 applied first):
  A(x,y) = (x^4 y + s(x^-1), x^-1), with s(x)=sum c_k x^-k, k in {2,3,4}.
  A is a morphism K[x,y] -> L^(1), not an automorphism of either ring.
  Its rational inverse is
    A^-1(u,v) = (v^-1, v^4 (u - s(v))).
  The first component v^-1 is not in L^(1)=K[u,u^-1,v] (it inverts the
  second variable). The paper gives no inverse formula and no description
  of the image A(K[x,y]) inside L^(1).
Which step introduces the denominator:
  Forward T5 introduces x^-1. In the inverse, undoing T5 introduces x^-1 in
  the first argument, which is allowed in L^(1). Undoing T1 (the swap) is the
  fatal step: it turns that x^-1 into y^-1, which is not in L^(1) and is not
  a polynomial. So the composite is not invertible on polynomial pairs; the
  inverse exists only as a rational map, defined on the image.
=== Stage 3 - implementation (problems/jacobian/reconstruct.py) ===
Created problems/jacobian/reconstruct.py (new file; no existing file modified).
The inverse chain is implemented exactly as A^-1(u,v)=(v^-1, v^4 (u-s(v))),
with s(v)=c2 v^-2 + c3 v^-3 + c4 v^-4, in three stages:
  step 1  undo phi:      (x,y) -> (x^-1, x^4 y)
  step 2  undo shears:   y -> y - (c2/x^2 + c3/x^3 + c4/x^4)
  step 3  undo swap:     (x,y) -> (y,x)
The script clears denominators using the minimal negative powers actually
present, computes det J exactly with sympy, and prints the stage at which the
result leaves L^(1)=K[x,x^-1,y].  A self-test runs the published forward
chain on P0=x, Q0=y: it gives det J = x^2 (the chain rule factor in Prop.
4.3) and the inverse chain recovers (x,y).  A positive control, the forward
image of the non-trivial polynomial pair (x+y^2, y), reconstructs exactly to
(x+y^2, y) with det J = 1.  So the procedure does reconstruct pairs that are
genuinely in the image; J3's failure below is not a bias of the code.

=== Stage 4 - J3 test ===
J3 family: P = a0 + a1*x, Q = b0 + b1*x^2*y, a1*b1 = 1, [P,Q]=x^2.
J3 is the vertices-only solution: N(P)={(0,0),(1,0)}, N(Q)={(0,0),(2,1)},
not either config-2 polygon.  It is the only explicit solution in
runs/jacobian_config2_vertices.json, hence the required stress test.  Its
failure does not by itself rule out a reconstructible pair with the full
config-2 polygon; that is why the verdict is PARTIAL, not IMPOSSIBLE.

Inverse stages:
  step 1: P = a0 + a1/x,                 Q = b0 + b1*x^2*y
          (Laurent in x, still in L^(1)).
  step 2: P = a0 + a1/x,
          Q = b0 - b1*c2 - b1*c3/x - b1*c4/x^2 + b1*x^2*y
          (still in L^(1)).
  step 3: P = a0 + a1/y,
          Q = b0 - b1*c2 - b1*c3/y - b1*c4/y^2 + b1*x*y^2
          (NOT in L^(1): y^-1 and y^-2 appear).

Negative powers after step 3:
  P: {-1: a1}
  Q: {-1: -b1*c3, -2: -b1*c4}

Rational round-trip check: applying the forward chain to the inverse pair
recovers P and Q exactly (forward(inverse(J3)) = J3).  So the inverse really
is a right inverse of the normalisation on L^(1); the failure is solely that
it does not land in K[x,y].

Before clearing: det J(P0,Q0) = a1*b1 = 1 exactly (the x^2 from the
normalized pair cancels det J(phi) = -x^2).

Cleared pair (multiply P by y, Q by y^2):
  P' = a0*y + a1
  Q' = b0*y^2 - b1*c2*y^2 - b1*c3*y - b1*c4 + b1*x*y^4
  det J(P',Q') = -a0*b1*y^4.
Under b1 = 1/a1 this is -a0*y^4/a1: zero if a0=0, a nonzero multiple of y^4
otherwise.  It is never a nonzero constant.

NOT RECONSTRUCTIBLE: step 3, the inverse swap turns the x^-1 introduced by
step 1 into y^-1.  P0 = a0 + a1/y is not a polynomial for any choice of
c2,c3,c4, because a1 != 0.  The shears act on the second coordinate and
cannot cancel a pole in the first coordinate.

No other polynomial clearing helps.  A polynomial representative has the
form (f*P0, g*Q0) with y | f and y^2 | g.  Since (P0)_x = 0 and a1*b1 = 1,
  det(f*P0, g*Q0) = g*(f - b1*y^2*f'*P0).
Writing f = y*h gives
  f - b1*y^2*f'*P0 = y*(h - b1*(a0*y+a1)*(h+y*h')),
so the determinant is divisible by y unless it is zero.  Hence no polynomial
clearing of the J3 pair can give a nonzero constant determinant.

=== Stage 5 - verdict ===
VERDICT: PARTIAL

Why PARTIAL rather than IMPOSSIBLE: the inverse formula itself is explicit
and exact, and every individual step is invertible on L^(1).  What is missing
is not the map but (a) the constants that select the branch, and (b) the
landing condition in K[x,y].  With those supplied, the reconstruction above
is a complete procedure.

Reason.
The inverse composite exists as a rational map and restores det J = 1 before
clearing, but it is not a map L^(1) -> K[x,y].  The paper's forward chain is
a morphism K[x,y] -> L^(1), not an automorphism of either ring: the swap T1
is an automorphism of K[x,y] but not of L^(1), and the monomial step T5
introduces x^-1.  In the inverse, the swap (step 3) turns that x^-1 into
y^-1, which is outside L^(1) and is not polynomial.  J3 fails at step 3 with
the exact cleared determinant -a0*b1*y^4.

The paper gives no explicit inverse of the composite and no characterization
of the image A(K[x,y]) inside L^(1).  The shear constants c2,c3,c4 are chosen
from the original polynomial P's leading forms, and the paper does not
express them in terms of the normalized pair.  A config-2 solution therefore
needs, in addition, the vanishing of every negative power of y in P0 and Q0.
Unlike J3, the config-2
lattice contains lower points such as (1,1), (1,2) and (5,b).  For a general
config-2 P the y^-1 coefficient is a nontrivial polynomial in c2,c3,c4 and
the lower coefficients; it can in principle be cancelled, but cancellation
is not automatic.  The deeper negative powers (down to y^-8 from the
(8,14),(8,16) vertices) give further conditions.  Prop. 4.3 states none of
them.

Additional data needed for a PARTIAL -> RECONSTRUCTION_EXISTS upgrade:
  (i) the exact shear constants, or the exact branch of the Prop. 4.3 proof
      that produced the normalized pair;
  (ii) the full polynomiality/image conditions - the vanishing of all
      negative powers of y in P0 and Q0 - imposed on the normalized
      coefficients;
  (iii) a check that the resulting pair lies in A(K[x,y]), i.e. that the
      config-2 solution is in the image of a polynomial pair.

Branch implication.
The config-2 coefficient system is necessary, not sufficient.  A solution of
det J = x^2 on the config-2 support is not a polynomial counterexample until
(i)-(iii) are supplied.  Do not run the (72,108) elimination expecting a
terminal object from the published normal form alone.  If the image
conditions are added and a solution survives, the reconstruction above gives
the candidate; if they force the system empty, that is a negative result
about the (72,108) case, not a counterexample.

